"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,529\nThe Learning Brain. Series 5: Belonging\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,416\nEpisode 11\n\n3\n00:00:07,496 --> 00:00:09,569\nImpostor feelings\n\n4\n00:00:12,369 --> 00:00:14,847\nEver feel that everyone else belongs here,\n\n5\n00:00:14,975 --> 00:00:16,679\nand you somehow slipped through?\n\n6\n00:00:17,122 --> 00:00:18,841\nThat one day you will be found out?\n\n7\n00:00:19,246 --> 00:00:22,491\nThat feeling is often called the impostor phenomenon.\n\n8\n00:00:22,661 --> 00:00:29,021\nThe idea goes back to psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes in 1978.\n\n9\n00:00:29,678 --> 00:00:34,239\nPeople with impostor feelings credit their success to luck or extra effort,\n\n10\n00:00:34,651 --> 00:00:38,458\nnot ability, and fear being exposed as a fraud.\n\n11\n00:00:38,628 --> 00:00:45,100\nIn 2020, Dena Bravata and colleagues systematically reviewed sixty two studies,\n\n12\n00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:47,833\nwith over fourteen thousand participants.\n\n13\n00:00:48,223 --> 00:00:50,696\nAbout half of the studies were of students.\n\n14\n00:00:50,866 --> 00:00:54,214\nReported rates ranged from nine to eighty two percent.\n\n15\n00:00:54,735 --> 00:00:58,843\nThat huge range depended mostly on which questionnaire was used,\n\n16\n00:00:59,061 --> 00:01:00,541\nand where the cutoff was set.\n\n17\n00:01:01,113 --> 00:01:04,823\nImpostor feelings were common in both men and women.\n\n18\n00:01:04,993 --> 00:01:07,848\nThey often came with depression and anxiety,\n\n19\n00:01:08,189 --> 00:01:11,266\nand were linked to burnout and poorer job performance.\n\n20\n00:01:11,436 --> 00:01:12,420\nSome caution.\n\n21\n00:01:12,769 --> 00:01:14,965\nIt is not a clinical diagnosis,\n\n22\n00:01:14,985 --> 00:01:16,794\nand the measures vary widely.\n\n23\n00:01:17,218 --> 00:01:19,648\nNo study in the review tested a treatment.\n\n24\n00:01:19,926 --> 00:01:21,956\nSo be wary of quick fixes.\n\n25\n00:01:22,126 --> 00:01:25,164\nImpostor feelings and belonging doubts overlap.\n\n26\n00:01:25,685 --> 00:01:29,061\nWhen students think they are the only one who feels this way,\n\n27\n00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:30,480\nthe doubt grows.\n\n28\n00:01:30,851 --> 00:01:33,031\nLearning that others share it can help,\n\n29\n00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:36,501\nas the belonging studies in episode one suggest.\n\n30\n00:01:36,671 --> 00:01:37,428\nFor teaching.\n\n31\n00:01:37,741 --> 00:01:41,766\nNormalize doubt: say that struggle and uncertainty are common,\n\n32\n00:01:41,786 --> 00:01:43,202\neven among experts.\n\n33\n00:01:43,546 --> 00:01:45,496\nShare your own early struggles.\n\n34\n00:01:45,778 --> 00:01:47,325\nPraise strategy and progress,\n\n35\n00:01:47,345 --> 00:01:49,087\nand make the standards clear.\n\n36\n00:01:49,545 --> 00:01:51,484\nOne tip to share with students: talk about your doubts,\n\n37\n00:01:51,688 --> 00:01:54,877\nbecause most classmates share them.\n\n38\n00:01:55,047 --> 00:01:58,927\nFeeling like an impostor is not evidence that you are one.\n\n"
